d":781659,"login":"jakevdp","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc4MTY1OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp","user_view_type":"public"},"assignees":[{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/781659?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jakevdp","id":781659,"login":"jakevdp","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc4MTY1OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp","user_view_type":"public"}],"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","body":"The `bcoo_extract` function can fail in situations where `true_nse < nse`. For example:\r\n```python\r\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\r\nfrom jax.experimental import sparse\r\n\r\nmat = jnp.ones((2, 3))\r\nmask = jnp.array([\r\n    [True, True, False],\r\n    [False, False, True]\r\n])\r\n\r\n# These two approaches should yield the same data array\r\ndata1, indices = sparse.bcoo_fromdense(mat.at[~mask].set(0), n_batch=1)\r\ndata2 = sparse.bcoo_extract(indices, mat)\r\nnp.testing.assert_allclose(data1, data2)\r\n# AssertionError: \r\n# Not equal to tolerance rtol=1e-07, atol=0\r\n\r\n# Mismatched elements: 1 / 4 (25%)\r\n# Max absolute difference: 1.\r\n# Max relative difference: 1.\r\n#  x: array([[1., 1.],\r\n#        [1., 0.]], dtype=float32)\r\n#  y: array([[1., 1.],\r\n#        [1., 1.]], dtype=float32)\r\n```\r\nThe issue arises here because `nse = 2` for the batched matrix, but there is only one nonzero entry in the second batch. The result is that the padded indices are treated as actual indices, and these values are erroneously extracted from the dense matrix.\r\n\r\nBecause `bcoo_extract` is used in a number of transform implementations within `jax.experimental.sparse`, this could potentially lead to some very subtle bugs, for example incorrect values in gradients of code involving `fromdense`.\r\n\r\nI think the best approach here would be to add another field to BCOO storage, a scalar `nse` which indicates the number of stored elements within the representation. This would be a dynamic value, and would play well with batching. This would change the call signature of all `bcoo_` primitives, but would be transparent to users of the `BCOO` class interface – this is a change I'd been considering anyway, but this bug makes it more of a necessity.","closed_at":"2021-10-18T19:20:09Z","closed_by":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/781659?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/jakevdp","id":781659,"login":"jakevdp","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjc4MTY1OQ==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jakevdp/repos","site_admin":false,"st